Go to pom.xml file and check tag, artifactId. Lets change it and compile and package the project. We can see that jar file name has also changed based on artifactId. This means jar file name is controlled by artifactId.
When we compile a project, using cmd, mvn compile package, by default test also runs even if we don't use test.
In the jar file name, we further see SNAPSHOT. We notice in pom.xml file that it has version as X.X.X-SNAPSHOT, it signifies that this jar file is still in development / testing phase. That's why it is termed as SNAPSHOT. Version has to do with the changes in our software. Any software will have 3 steps of versions provided. One is known as critical changes meaning we have done critical changes like adding many features, updation etc. The first zero in version specifies that. To represent the critical changes, we change the first zero to one. If we have done some minor or major changes not critical changes, then second zero will be increased by 1. If any minor change happens , then third zero is increased by 1. Once our project is completely developed and tested and is ready for deployment in production server, then version changes from SNAPSHOT to RELEASE. When we change the version from SNAPSHOT to RELEASE and compile the project, jar file name also changes from SNAPSHOT to RELEASE. jar file name is combination of artifact id and version. From the jar file , any developer can understand if it is ready for production deployment or not and accordingly we can find RELEASE branch in github which have production ready code in it.

