What is Dependency Exclusion?

- Dependency Exclusion in Maven is a mechanism that allows us to remove unwanted transitive dependencies that are being pulled into our project. These transitive dependencies are usually automatically included when we add a library (dependency) to our project. However, sometimes we might not need all of them.

- By excluding unnecessary dependencies, you can reduce the size of your project and avoid conflicts with other dependencies.

Breakdown:
The Parent Dependency:

-spring-context is added as a direct dependency in our project.
- When we add this dependency, Maven automatically resolves its transitive dependencies like spring-core, spring-beans, spring-aop, spring-expression, and spring-jcl.
Transitive Dependencies:
- These are transitive dependencies because they are required by spring-context, and Maven resolves them automatically.
Excluding a Transitive Dependency (spring-aop):
- Sometimes, we might not need all of these transitive dependencies. In this case, we might not want to include spring-aop in our project.
- The <exclusions> tag is used to exclude spring-aop.
- This ensures that when we include spring-context, Maven will not include the spring-aop dependency in our project, even though it's a transitive dependency of spring-context.

Advantage
- Reduced Project Size
- Improved Build time

Without exclusion

<dependency>
    <groupId>org.springframework</groupId>
    <artifactId>spring-context</artifactId>
    <version>6.2.0</version>
</dependency>

With exclusion

<dependency>
    <groupId>org.springframework</groupId>
    <artifactId>spring-context</artifactId>
    <version>6.2.0</version>
    <exclusions>
        <exclusion>
            <groupId>org.springframework</groupId>
            <artifactId>spring-aop</artifactId>
        </exclusion>
    </exclusions>
</dependency>


